<Application
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    x:Class="DesktopOrganizer.UI.App"
    Startup="StartApp"
    ShutdownMode="OnExplicitShutdown">
    <!--
        Ambient Accent Visual System (ADR-025, docs/PLANS/phase-5-ambient-accent-ui.md §1 D-1, D-12):
        Application.Resources' merge chain (Colors -> Spacing -> Brushes -> Typography -> Icons ->
        Controls -> Panels -> Templates, with Colors.HighContrast.xaml inserted conditionally) is built
        entirely in code — App.xaml.cs's LoadResourceDictionaries(), called once at startup — NOT
        declared here as a static <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries> list.

        Reason: Brushes.xaml's entries are plain `<SolidColorBrush ... po:Freeze="True"/>` objects, not
        deferred content the way Style/ControlTemplate/DataTemplate resources are (WPF only defers THOSE
        types' parsing until first lookup) — so each brush, and the `{StaticResource Color.*}` reference
        inside it, is resolved as soon as Brushes.xaml itself is parsed/merged, not lazily on first
        consumer access, and `po:Freeze="True"` then locks that resolved value into an immutable Brush
        instance that can never change afterwards. Whether the high-contrast Colors override is in effect
        has to be decided — and Colors.HighContrast.xaml merged in, ahead of Brushes.xaml — BEFORE that
        parse happens; a fixed, statically-ordered list declared here has no way to conditionally insert a
        dictionary partway through itself. See LoadResourceDictionaries()'s own remarks for the exact
        order (unchanged from before this comment, for the non-high-contrast case) and reasoning.

        Do not add <Application.Resources> back here without re-reading that method.
    -->
</Application>
